## Method and reasoning
Let dp[v] be the least weight that achieves exact value v. Start only value zero as feasible and scan values downward per item so that item cannot enter twice. Find the largest value whose minimum weight is at most W.

## Walk through the sample
Value 11 is attainable at weight 6 using the value-7 and value-4 items. Value 12 needs the value-5 and value-7 items at weight 7. Testing minimum weight for each value selects 11 at capacity 6.

## Complexity
Time O(n·sumValues), space O(sumValues).

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    int n;
    long long capacity;
    cin >> n >> capacity;
    vector<pair<long long, int>> items(n);
    int sum = 0;
    for (auto &[w, v] : items) {
        cin >> w >> v;
        sum += v;
    }
    const long long inf = LLONG_MAX / 4;
    vector<long long> cost(sum + 1, inf);
    cost[0] = 0;
    int top = 0;
    for (auto [w, v] : items) {
        top += v;
        for (int x = top; x >= v; --x)
            cost[x] = min(cost[x], cost[x - v] + w);
    }
    while (cost[sum] > capacity)
        --sum;
    cout << sum << '\n';
}
